上游与第三方组件 / Upstream & Third-Party Components
================================================================================

Aurivox is a local-first TTS workbench that integrates, adapts, or contains
components derived from upstream and third-party projects. Their respective
licenses and notices apply to their code, model weights, and other materials
as applicable.

- GPT-SoVITS
  https://github.com/RVC-Boss/GPT-SoVITS

  Licensed under the MIT License. Portions of Aurivox's training pipeline and
  inference engine are adapted from or derived from GPT-SoVITS
  (v2 / v2Pro / v2ProPlus).

- Third-party model weights

  Third-party model weights (including GPT-SoVITS pretrained models,
  speech-recognition models, and vocal-separation models) are not bundled with
  the standard Aurivox distribution. If a separate distribution includes any
  such weights, that distribution must identify them explicitly and comply with
  their applicable licenses.

  The deployment wizard may help users obtain selected components from the
  upstream or distribution sources identified in the model manifest
  (THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES/models/MODEL_SOURCES.json). Those components remain governed by their respective
  licenses.

  Aurivox does not grant rights on behalf of third-party copyright holders.
  The deployment wizard does not determine whether a third-party component is
  suitable for commercial use or any other particular use.
  Anyone who downloads, bundles, uses, modifies, or redistributes a third-party
  component is responsible for reviewing and complying with the license
  applicable to that component.

- Bundled runtimes

  * CPython / python-build-standalone
    Governed by the PSF License and the licenses applicable to bundled runtime
    components (which may include OpenSSL, SQLite, libffi, and others). Refer to
    the license materials supplied with the selected python-build-standalone
    distribution.

  * Node.js
    Governed by the MIT License and the licenses applicable to its bundled
    third-party components.

- Bundled Python wheels

  Aurivox's Python runtime dependencies are installed at deploy time with
  pip / uv from a package index; they are NOT redistributed inside the Aurivox
  distribution. The complete frozen dependency set is recorded for
  reproducibility in the project's requirements file (requirements.txt), but
  those packages are fetched by the end user's own installer, not shipped by us.

  The distribution package bundles only the following prebuilt Python wheels,
  which are shipped because prebuilt binaries for the target platform are not
  reliably available from the index:

  * jieba_fast (jieba_fast-0.53-cp311-cp311-win_amd64.whl)
    Licensed under the MIT License.

  * pyopenjtalk (pyopenjtalk-0.4.1-cp311-cp311-win_amd64.whl)
    The Python wrapper is licensed under the MIT License. The wheel also bundles
    the modified OpenJTalk and hts_engine_API, which are governed by their own
    (modified BSD-style) licenses; those texts travel with the wheel.

  Their license texts are preserved under THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES/. Each remains
  the property of its respective authors; Aurivox does not relicense them and
  does not grant any rights on behalf of their copyright holders.

  All other Python dependencies (including PyTorch, which is installed
  separately from a CUDA-specific package index) are obtained by the installer
  from their respective sources and remain governed by their own licenses.
  The full inventory is recorded in THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES/runtime/python_packages.json.

- Media tools obtained separately (not bundled)

  * FFmpeg (ffmpeg, ffprobe)
    Aurivox does NOT redistribute FFmpeg. A provisioning script
    (tools/deploy/download_ffmpeg.py) fetches the static ffmpeg/ffprobe binaries
    at deploy time from their upstream distributor (BtbN win64-gpl build; Aurivox
    is Windows-only) into a project-local vendor directory. That build is
    GPL-licensed (GPL-3.0-or-later; it does not use --enable-nonfree), and the
    GPL license text and corresponding-source obligations travel with the
    upstream download and rest with that distributor, not with Aurivox.
    Aurivox invokes FFmpeg as a separate process (not by linking), so FFmpeg's
    license does not affect Aurivox's own MIT license. The source/license
    inventory is recorded in THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES/EXTERNAL_TOOLS.json.

Corresponding third-party license texts and notices are preserved under
THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES/ in the distribution where required. Exact model sources,
revisions, and license references are recorded separately in the project model
manifest (THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES/models/MODEL_SOURCES.json).

